WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.180
In Season 2 of Chucky, there is a scene where&nbsp;
the kids gather materials that might help them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.720
reprogram Chucky’s brain, and if you look&nbsp;
closely, one of these materials is a tape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.140
that once belonged to Eddie Caputo, a boy&nbsp;
who attended this very same boarding house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:14.280
years ago and later went on to become Chucky’s&nbsp;
getaway driver, as seen in the original movie.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.600
Not only is this a fun easter egg, but it also&nbsp;
establishes the idea that Charles and Eddie may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.920
have items tucked away in hidden places throughout&nbsp;
the school, an idea that comes back in a huge way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:27.360
at the end of this season. To learn about all&nbsp;
the easter eggs, references, hidden secrets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.740
glaring mistakes and everything you might have&nbsp;
missed, stick around to the end of this video.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.320
This video is sponsored by Hello Fresh

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.400
(metal intro)

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.117
Welcome to Things You Missed.

00:00:43.083 --> 00:00:46.320
(melancholic piano music)

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.680
OK, if you watch my videos, you may&nbsp;
remember how relieved I was when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.400
was finally done covering Bride and Seed. I&nbsp;
only did it so that my Chucky series could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.300
be considered complete and we could move&nbsp;
onto the serious stuff. Then, in Season 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:01.080
the main focus was how that season was a&nbsp;
tribute to Child’s Play 2, which creator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:05.040
Don Mancini explained was because so many fans&nbsp;
consider Child’s Play 2 to be their favorite.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:10.260
My hope was that Season 2 would take the next&nbsp;
logical step and honor Child’s Play 3. Afterall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.500
like Child’s Play 3, it was going to&nbsp;
take place at a boarding school. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.200
it would feature a small militia&nbsp;
of Chucky dolls opposing Andy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.920
the inverse of Child’s Play 3 which features&nbsp;
a small militia of kids opposing Chucky.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:25.140
However, as I watched Season 2, I came to&nbsp;
a horrifying realization. This wasn’t an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:31.058
homage to the third movie… it was an homage to&nbsp;
the fifth one. Seed of Chucky. (horror sting)

00:01:31.058 --> 00:01:36.000
As a result, Chucky Season 2 throws horror out the&nbsp;
window, pun intended, and it’s all about comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.160
gross out moments, celebrity cameos, meta-horror&nbsp;
references and way too much petty bickering.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:44.880
I’ve got to commend Mancini for having&nbsp;
the balls to do this. I’m sure he knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:49.080
how fans feel about Seed, and yet he stuck&nbsp;
to his guns and gave us more of it. Four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:52.500
times more. Regardless of my feelings&nbsp;
towards this season, I still feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.980
it contains some interesting things you&nbsp;
may have missed. So let’s get into them.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:01.500
(rock music)

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.960
The season picks up right where&nbsp;
the previous one left off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.800
with Andy hijacking the truck full of Chuck in&nbsp;
order to try and destroy them. It’s here that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.580
we meet the doll that would later become known&nbsp;
as The Colonel. He gets into it with two others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.820
and I have a fan theory about who&nbsp;
one of them is. Listen to this line.

00:02:16.205 --> 00:02:19.620
– [The Colonel] They also say&nbsp;
it ain’t the size that counts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:24.420
So I wouldn’t worry about those&nbsp;
pathetic stick arms of yours.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.960
– My theory is that this insult&nbsp;
inspired this Chucky to take steroids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.380
resulting in the creation of one of&nbsp;
this season’s antagonists: Buff Chucky.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:34.500
As soon as I saw Andy drive the truck&nbsp;
over the ledge, I was screaming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:40.320
“he didn’t get out of the cockodoodie truck!” Come&nbsp;
to find out, he actually didn’t for some reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.160
he just somehow miraculously survived the&nbsp;
crash, which happens right at the foot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.580
this giant cross, perhaps foreshadowing&nbsp;
the religious setting for this season.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.340
Season 2 Episode 1 is titled “Halloween II”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.820
because it’s the second CHUCKY episode&nbsp;
that takes place on Halloween. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:57.780
there is a double meaning here, because there&nbsp;
are going to be similarities to the 1981 movie:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:02.280
Halloween II. The jack o’lanterns in the intro&nbsp;
have Chucky’s face carved into them in the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.720
style as the skull on the poster for Halloween&nbsp;
II. During the episode we see that Jake carved one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.100
of Chucky-lanterns himself. I… don’t know why he&nbsp;
would traumatize himself like that. But he does.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:15.180
As I mentioned in my video on Season 1,&nbsp;
Dr. Mixter is named after a character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.260
from Halloween II. So these references&nbsp;
may be our clue that Dr. Mixter is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:22.020
to play a bigger role this season. A&nbsp;
role that would make her align more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:26.220
with a different doctor from the Halloween&nbsp;
franchise: Dr. Sartain, the psychiatrist of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:30.060
a serial killer who becomes so invested in&nbsp;
his patient, that he becomes evil himself.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.840
But from Bride of Chucky onwards, I’m&nbsp;
pretty sure all of my Chucky analysys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.080
have made mention of the horror movie&nbsp;
franchise that Chucky takes on the most:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:41.820
The Scream franchise. This season may be the&nbsp;
most blatant Scream emulation of all. It starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:45.480
with Jake receiving a cell phone call from&nbsp;
Chucky, doing his best Ghostface impression.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.600
– [Jake] Hello?
– [Chucky] Is Tamara home?&nbsp;

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.220
– Who?
– Tamara?

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:53.280
– This is actually kind of a hybrid&nbsp;
easter egg. The phone call and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.400
voice resemble the beginning of Scream,&nbsp;
but the line is actually the line from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.508
the beginning of The Strangers, when one of&nbsp;
the titular strangers shows up at the door.

00:04:00.508 --> 00:04:02.587
– Is Tamara here?

00:04:02.587 --> 00:04:04.951
– No, you’ve got the wrong house.

00:04:04.951 --> 00:04:08.340
– The rest of the phone call plays out like a&nbsp;
typical Scream opening, with Chucky asking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:12.360
him questions such as why he’s home alone,&nbsp;
revealing familiarity with Jake by asking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:16.740
about his significant other, then frightening
Jake by proving that real danger is afoot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:21.300
The doorbell rings, but no one is out front, and&nbsp;
Jake grabs a knife. It’s all just like Scream.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:25.260
At first, he thinks it’s a classmate named&nbsp;
Ellis, which may be a reference to Major Elllis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:28.440
the bully character from Child’s&nbsp;
Play 3. We’ve seen this move before.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.220
– [Chucky] This isn’t Ellis…
– [Ghostface] I’m not Randy.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:35.760
– The Screaminess continues later in&nbsp;
the episode when Devon recognizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:37.128
Caroline's new doll from something.

00:04:37.128 --> 00:04:38.940
– [Devon] She looks a little&nbsp;
like the Bride of Chucky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.780
Remember that movie?
– [Jake] Yeah, with Jennifer Tilly.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:46.620
– We do know that there are movies about Chucky&nbsp;
already, because part of Seed took place on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:50.460
the set of a movie called Chucky Goes Psycho.&nbsp;
However, this is the first time we’re hearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:54.780
that the actual Chucky movies in our world,&nbsp;
may also exist in their world, which is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:58.980
concept that originated in the Scream franchise,&nbsp;
which contains the in-universe Stab franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:03.660
In addition to Chucky Goes Psycho and Bride of&nbsp;
Chucky, it seems Seed of Chucky also exists in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:07.560
some form in the Chucky universe, because you can&nbsp;
spot a poster for it in Jennifer Tilly’s house.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:09.960
The show continues to lean&nbsp;
heavily on Scream throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.360
the season. Nica borrows a line from Casey Becker…

00:05:12.434 --> 00:05:14.325
– Listen ***hole!

00:05:15.133 --> 00:05:16.578
– Listen ***hole!

00:05:16.578 --> 00:05:17.280
– [Ghostface] No you!

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:20.820
– [Zac] The rest of these will contain spoilers for&nbsp;
Scream, so just skip to the next chapter if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:24.420
you haven’t seen them. In Episode 3, we learn&nbsp;
that there are two Chucky’s operating at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:27.600
the school, just as there are always two&nbsp;
Ghostface killers in every Scream movie.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.360
In Episode 8, Glenda kills&nbsp;
someone with a defibrillator…

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:34.043
– Hey Mom? Clear.

00:05:34.918 --> 00:05:36.000
(electricity) (screaming)

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.670
– … very much like in Scream 4…

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:41.880
– Can I just have one final word?&nbsp;

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:43.839
– What? Please?

00:05:43.839 --> 00:05:45.124
– No.

00:05:47.225 --> 00:05:48.766
Clear.

00:05:48.766 --> 00:05:49.433
– Clear?

00:05:49.433 --> 00:05:50.767
– [Sidney] Clear!

00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:54.900
– Note, that one might actually be more of a&nbsp;
reference to the first Child’s Play, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:59.100
Chucky kills Dr. Ardmore with some kind of other&nbsp;
shock device at the hospital. But like Scream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:04.260
the Chucky franchise goes to New York City,&nbsp;
which is possibly a really big coincidence.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:08.340
However, the aspect of Scream that is most&nbsp;
borrowed by Chucky is the fact that the characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:13.200
are self aware and know about horror movies. The&nbsp;
season is littered with parodies and homages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:17.520
so I’m gonna talk about most of them,&nbsp;
and uncover even more Things You Missed.

